{{smaller block|Ther with the nyghtspel seyde he anon rightes<br>On foure halves of the hous aboute,<br>And on the ''thresshfold'' of the dore withoute.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Miller's Tale, l. 296.}}
Still at hell's dark ''threshold'' to have sat watch.
{{right|''Milton'', P. L., x. 594.}}
Forward leaped she o'er the ''threshold'',<br>Eager as a glancing surf. ''Lowell'', The Captive.}}

'''2.''' Hence, the place or point of entering or beginning; outset: as, he is now at the ''threshold'' of his argument.

{{smaller block|The fair new forms<br>That float about the ''threshold'' of an age,<br>Like truths of Science waiting to be caught.
{{right|''Tennyson'', Golden Year.}}}}

'''3.''' In ''psychol.'', the limit below which a given stimulus, or the difference between two stimuli, ceases to be perceptible. Compare ''schwelle''.—'''Dweller on the threshold.''' {{smaller|See ''dweller''.}}—'''Stimulus threshold.''' {{smaller|See ''stimulus''.}}

'''threshwold†, thresshfold†,''' ''n.'' Middle English forms of ''threshold''.

